WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.437
Use this checkpoint to test whether you understand the reasoning, not only the SQL.

00:00:04.437 --> 00:00:06.137
Explain why NULL differs from blank text.

00:00:06.137 --> 00:00:11.952
Explain why an invalid value should be detected before it is corrected.

00:00:11.952 --> 00:00:15.592
Explain why duplicate detection and survivor selection are separate.

00:00:15.592 --> 00:00:18.627
Explain the limits of regex validation.

00:00:18.627 --> 00:00:20.982
And explain why a transaction makes cleaning updates safer.

00:00:20.982 --> 00:00:25.392
For this activity, make a prediction before you run anything.

00:00:25.392 --> 00:00:29.807
State what one output row should represent, which records you expect to remain,

00:00:29.807 --> 00:00:31.647
and what edge case is most likely to expose a mistake.

00:00:31.647 --> 00:00:35.737
Then run the SQL and compare the result with that prediction.

00:00:35.737 --> 00:00:40.877
If it does not match, debug the logic in small pieces instead of changing several clauses at once.

00:00:40.877 --> 00:00:47.972
This is the same habit you would use in a real analytics project: validate the meaning of the result,

00:00:47.972 --> 00:00:49.840
not only whether the statement executes.
